[Bitbucket](https://bitbucket.org) is Atlassian's Git hosting service. Teams organize repositories into workspaces, review changes in pull requests, and link their work to Jira, which makes Bitbucket a common home for code at companies that already run on Atlassian tools.

# How does the integration work?

Bitbucket is one of Appwrite's Git providers for [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites) and [Appwrite Functions](/docs/products/functions). You connect Bitbucket to your Appwrite project once, through Bitbucket's OAuth consent screen. Every workspace your account can reach appears as a selectable owner in the repository picker, next to owners from any other Git provider you have connected.

Each push to a connected repository can create a deployment:

- **Production branch:** the deployment builds and activates.
- **Any other branch:** the deployment builds but stays inactive. Sites get a [preview link](/docs/products/sites/previews) that only members of your Appwrite organization can open.
- **Every build:** Appwrite reports a commit status on Bitbucket and comments on the pull request with the deployment status, build logs, preview URL, and a QR code.

Commit, branch, and pull request links on each deployment resolve to Bitbucket's own URL shapes.

{% info title="Self-hosted Appwrite" %}
Self-hosted Appwrite 2.0 and later can deploy from Bitbucket once you give the server a Bitbucket OAuth consumer. Appwrite connects to Bitbucket Cloud at `bitbucket.org`. See [Configure Bitbucket on self-hosted Appwrite](#configure-bitbucket-on-self-hosted-appwrite).
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To deploy from Bitbucket to Appwrite, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Connect Bitbucket to your Appwrite project

For this step, you need to [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) with Bitbucket configured.

In the Appwrite Console, open your project, go to **Project settings** > **Git configuration**, and click **Connect Bitbucket**. Approve Bitbucket's OAuth consent screen, and the connection is saved to the project. Its repositories are available immediately.

You can also connect from an existing site or function under **Settings** > **Version control (Git)**, or from the empty state of the site and function creation wizards when the project has no Git connection yet.

## Step 2: Create a Site or Function from a Bitbucket repository

To deploy a site, go to **Sites** and click **Create site**. Under **Connect Git repository**, pick your Bitbucket workspace, select the repository, and confirm the framework, install command, build command, and output directory. Add any environment variables, then deploy.

To deploy a function, go to **Functions** and click **Create function**. Under **Connect Git repository**, select your Bitbucket repository, set the root directory and entrypoint, and create the function.

## Step 3: Push to deploy

Push to your production branch, usually `main`, to build and activate a new deployment. Push to any other branch to get a deployment that builds without activating, along with a preview link that Appwrite posts on the pull request.

[Build triggers](/docs/products/sites/deploy-from-git#build-triggers) limit which pushes deploy. Branch filters match branch names, and path filters match changed files:

```txt
main
staging
preview/**
```

A project can hold Bitbucket and GitHub or GitLab connections at the same time, so different Sites and Functions in one project can deploy from different providers.

# Configure Bitbucket on self-hosted Appwrite

Self-hosted Appwrite needs its own Bitbucket OAuth consumer before it can connect to Bitbucket. To add one:

1. In Bitbucket, open your workspace, select the **Settings** cog, then **Workspace settings**. Under **Apps and features**, select **OAuth consumers** and click **Add consumer**.
2. Set the **Callback URL** to `https://<APPWRITE_DOMAIN>/v1/vcs/bitbucket/callback`, where `<APPWRITE_DOMAIN>` is your `_APP_CONSOLE_DOMAIN`, or `_APP_DOMAIN` when that is not set. Use `http://` only if `_APP_OPTIONS_FORCE_HTTPS` is `disabled`.
3. Grant the permissions behind the scopes Appwrite requests: `account`, `repository:write`, `pullrequest:write`, and `webhook`. Save the consumer and copy its **Key** and **Secret**.
4. Add the credentials to your Appwrite `.env` file:

```env
_APP_VCS_BITBUCKET_CLIENT_ID=<KEY>
_APP_VCS_BITBUCKET_CLIENT_SECRET=<SECRET>
_APP_VCS_BITBUCKET_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<RANDOM_STRING>
```

The webhook secret can be any random string: Appwrite uses it when it registers a webhook on each repository you connect, and to validate the events Bitbucket sends to `/v1/vcs/bitbucket/events`. Bitbucket must be able to reach your Appwrite domain. If your public address differs from `_APP_DOMAIN`, set `_APP_VCS_WEBHOOK_URL` to the base URL Bitbucket should call.

Recreate the containers and check that the variables are applied:

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose exec appwrite vars
```

